{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>When a fire goes out, etc.</b> Meaning: even though he kindled it within his own property, when the fire that was kindled has enough measure to go out to a place that is not his. And the measure of the word <b>תֵּצֵא</b> is that it is fit to go out; but if he kindled within his own property and there was not enough measure to go out outside, yet it went out — he is exempt, for this is a blow by the hands of Heaven, as is found in the Gemara (Bava Kamma 61a). And its saying <b>and it finds thorns</b> — meaning: even though beneath the thorns there was not enough to reach the standing grain or the stack to ignite them, yet through the medium of the thorns the stack, etc., was consumed — <b>he shall surely pay</b>: meaning, the full value of the stack or the standing grain he shall pay; the value of the wood [i.e., the thorns] he shall [also] pay, even though they were on the contrary the cause of the damage. And specifically when it found them [i.e., the thorns were already there]; but if a person came and placed them after the ignition — the second one is liable to pay." "<b>The one who ignites.</b> And it repeated to say <b>הַבְּעֵרָה</b>, to allude also to the wood [thorns], that they are what ignite the stack and the standing grain." "<b>Further,</b> it intends by saying <b>and it finds</b>: even though the one who ignites did not know that thorns were there, or even if they were not there at the time of the ignition, and a person came and placed them in his area and did not know that there is an ignition — behold, the one who ignites is liable." "<b>And by way of</b> allusion, the Torah alludes to that which the wicked stir up in their wickedness, so that a person may awaken from the slumber of his mind's sleep when he observes the multiplicity of evils that they bring upon the world, for not only do they do evil [to themselves] but they stir up evil for the entire world. And this is its saying <b>when a fire goes out</b>: do you not know that the distress of the people is called by the name 'fire'? Go and learn from the dispatching of Gabriel (Yoma 77a), who requested fire from between the staves (the cherubim), and this is the aspect of the intensification of the judgments." "And it says <b>and it finds thorns</b> — these are the wicked, who are nothing but painful thorns. And it uses the language of <b>מָצְאָה</b> in the manner of its saying (Deuteronomy 31:21) ״וְהָיָה כִּי תִמְצֶאןָ אֹתוֹ״ etc., and the reason is because the wicked has no master, for the Master, blessed is He, has declared it ownerless [הִנֵּה הוּא פָּקַר בּוֹ] and removed His lordship from over him, and HaShem has hidden His face from him; and from now on he is like a found object, and whoever encounters him may do with him as he wishes. And the verse further informs that after permission has been given to the fire of the judgments to ignite, they do not ignite the thorns alone, but <b>and the stack is consumed</b> — here it alluded to the schoolchildren, who do not yet have 'standing' [קוֹמָה, independent stature] but are [like] gathered sheaves of grain with their fellows, for in times of calamity they are the ones caught in the world's distress. They also said, their memory for a blessing (Shir HaShirim Rabbah 1:4), on the verse (Psalms 8:3) ״מִפִּי עוֹלְלִים וְיוֹנְקִים יִסַּדְתָּ עֹז״ — for they were guarantors at the time of the receiving of the Torah, and when the children of Israel act wickedly, behold permission is given to the fire to consume them." "And the verse's saying <b>or the standing grain</b>: here it informs that sometimes the sword reaches the very neck — the wonders of the generation, the foundations of the world, are taken in their sin in order to save the entirety of the generation, as it says (Song of Songs 1:14) ״אֶשְׁכֹּל הַכֹּפֶר״ etc., and they are called קָמָה in the manner of the verse (Song of Songs 7:8) ״זֹאת קוֹמָתֵךְ דָּמְתָה לְתָמָר״ — for these are the righteous who are likened to a palm tree, as it says (Psalms 92:13) ״צַדִּיק כַּתָּמָר״ etc. And sometimes the sin grows so great that the righteous alone cannot save, and wrath is poured out upon all the enemies of Israel, and this is the verse's saying <b>or the field</b>, which alludes to the entirety of Israel, and neither infants nor righteous ones suffice to save." "And the verse informs that there is a day for El, awesome to come in judgment against those who surround the matter — the bringing about of the death of the righteous and the giants of the world — to exact recompense from them, and this is the verse's saying <b>he shall surely make full restitution, the one who kindles the fire</b> — these are the wicked, the thorns, for all whom they have killed. And they said, may their memory be for blessing (Sanhedrin 100a), that HaShem gives power to the wicked to bear the measure that reaches them through their sin — woe to the wicked, evil for the heavens and for the creatures, how much he will suffer on account of his corruption. And you will find (Genesis Rabbah 63:10) that the righteous, when their children reached the age of years such that they would no longer be caught in the father's sin, would rejoice and say: Blessed is the One who released them from this punishment — see, when HaShem punishes the one who causes punishment, it is on his account for another. And truly, a man should tremble and extinguish the flame of his impulse that makes him sin — with the fire of judgment, when he brings upon his heart the rivers of punishments that will reach him for how many souls, small and great, without end, all of whom he is the great murderer through the agency of his putrid desire. And behold, they said, may their memory be for blessing (Kiddushin 40b): A person should always regard himself as though the entire world were half guilty etc. — if he commits one transgression, woe to him, for he has tipped the entire world toward guilt, and the blood of the entire world and the blood of their seed is upon his neck and loaded upon his soul, and he will endure the bitterness of death for every single soul among them, and in all their distress he shall be distressed, as he did. And this is the verse's saying <b>he shall surely make full restitution</b> etc. — the reason for the doubling of שַׁלֵּם etc. <b>the one who kindles</b> etc.: here the verse alludes that HaShem will execute the judgment of restitution not upon the wicked man alone but also upon the one who causes him to sin — and that is the evil impulse, the force that causes him to sin, for it too has a harsh decree on that day; great will be the lamentation over it when HaShem executes judgment upon it, as they said, may their memory be for blessing (Sukkah 52a), that HaShem brings it and slaughters it, and it will not escape by fleeing to a fortress or even to a fortress-refuge (Makkot 12a), for it is intentional, and the King of judgment shall judge in righteousness to exact recompense from it, and HaShem alone shall be exalted. And in Bava Kamma (60a) they alluded in another manner."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:32:51.950367904Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}